- in_text_citation: [(Hayashi et al., 2015-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cfea4efde550cf5ae458eae2efe0fc303f713623)
- venue: PLoS Biology
- citation count: 98
[method]
The paper used an fMRI adaptation approach to examine neural responses to repeated visual stimulus durations. Experiments 1 and 2 compared durations of 400 and 600 ms, while Experiment 4 compared durations of 300 and 450 ms. Participants performed a time task in which duration was task-relevant and a shape task in which duration was task-irrelevant, and Experiment 3 used a graded shape discrimination task to test shape repetition. The analysis focused on adaptation effects in the IPL and PTC. This method is related to the proposed fMRI study of temporal duration processing, but it tests duration adaptation rather than egocentric versus allocentric temporal sequence representations or parametric modulation of sequential distance.
[results]
The provided excerpt reports qualitative outcomes only and does not include quantitative effect sizes, statistical thresholds, or behavioral performance values. It reports that duration adaptation occurred in the right IPL during the time task, that this duration adaptation was replicated with 300 and 450 ms durations, and that duration adaptation occurred regardless of whether duration was task-relevant. It also reports no adaptation to shape repetition in the IPL and PTC, including in the right IPL and PTC during the graded shape discrimination task.
[context]
The experimental context was an fMRI adaptation design using short visual stimulus durations of 400/600 ms and 300/450 ms, a time task, a shape task, and a graded shape discrimination task. The key regions of interest were the IPL and PTC, with a specific replication finding in the right IPL. The provided excerpt does not specify participant numbers, scanner parameters, preprocessing details, statistical thresholds, or behavioral metrics, so those reproducibility details are unavailable.
[end related work 1]

