- in_text_citation: [(Hayashi et al., 2018-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c3d9101426abdb68a07d4f49760f77c5b1d795cf)
- venue: Communications Biology
- citation count: 48
[method]
The paper used fMRI to examine duration processing in a duration task, with functional localizer scans and main scans. GLM analyses were applied to both localizer and main scan data, and searchlight MVPA was used to decode duration and orientation information from frontal, parietal, and occipital ROIs. Behavioral performance was also assessed during localizer and main duration-task scans. This method is related to the proposed fMRI investigation of duration processing, but it appears to focus on duration and orientation decoding rather than the proposed internal versus external temporal perspectives, sequential distance, or hippocampal-parietal complementarity.
[results]
The available section headings indicate that frontal and parietal ROIs carried duration information, that an occipital ROI carried orientation information, and that duration information was present in frontoparietal and occipital regions. Decoding accuracy was compared between time and orientation. No numerical decoding accuracies, behavioral accuracy or reaction-time values, effect sizes, or statistical significance values are provided in the supplied text.
[context]
The experimental context included functional localizer scans, a main duration task, behavioral data analysis, MRI data acquisition, GLM modeling for localizer and main scans, searchlight MVPA, and ROI analyses of frontal, parietal, and occipital regions. The supplied text does not specify participant numbers, scanner field strength, sequence parameters, trial structure, duration values, or statistical correction thresholds.
[end related work 2]

[start related work 1]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Baker et al., 2015-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/beae3f2b36080c965b214dd5fa1fcaec650dc548)
- venue: NeuroImage
- citation count: 50
[method]
The paper used seed-based resting-state fMRI connectivity analysis rather than parametric modulation. Two 2 mm spherical seeds were placed in the right anterior superior parietal lobule and right posterior superior parietal lobule, and their time series were extracted from resting-state fMRI. For each participant, whole-brain connectivity maps were generated by using each seed time series as an explanatory variable in a subject-level regression, with nuisance regressors for white matter, cerebrospinal fluid, and six motion parameters. White matter and cerebrospinal fluid time courses were derived from individual structural segmentation and tissue probability maps, thresholded at 66 percent for white matter and 40 percent for cerebrospinal fluid, and binarized. Group-level analysis used mixed-effects modeling with automatic outlier detection and mean displacement as a covariate. The resulting connectivity maps were then regressed against a temporal duration variable, specifically the geometric mean dominance duration for binocular rivalry and Necker cube percepts. This method relates to the proposed research method and plan because it uses fMRI to identify parietal and temporal regions whose neural signal covaries with a temporal duration variable, but it differs from the proposed approach because it uses resting-state connectivity and a single continuous duration measure per stimulus rather than trial-level parametric modulation of sequential distance, event duration, and perspective.
[results]
In a resting-state sample of 141 participants, the anterior superior parietal lobule seed was connected to occipital, parietal, and dorsal temporal regions, while the posterior superior parietal lobule seed was connected to temporo-parietal, temporal, and dorsolateral prefrontal regions. In an independent sample of 26 participants, geometric mean dominance durations for binocular rivalry and the Necker cube correlated at r = 0.46 with p < 0.05. Connectivity between the anterior superior parietal lobule seed and striatal regions was associated with faster binocular rivalry alternations, meaning shorter dominance durations. Connectivity between the posterior superior parietal lobule seed and left inferior temporal gyrus was associated with slower binocular rivalry alternations, meaning longer dominance durations. Connectivity between the anterior superior parietal lobule seed and a left medial region at the boundary between pre-supplementary motor area and dorsal anterior cingulate cortex was associated with slower Necker cube alternations. Connectivity associations were identified using Z > 2.3 and corrected cluster-level p < 0.05, with fMRI beta parameter estimates plotted against dominance durations.
[context]
The resting-state fMRI data were preprocessed in FSL with skull stripping, registration to standard space, motion correction, slice-timing correction, non-brain removal, 6 mm Gaussian spatial smoothing, grand-mean intensity normalization, highpass temporal filtering with sigma = 100 s, and lowpass temporal filtering with sigma = 2.8 s. Seed regions were 2 mm in radius. Group inference used FLAME stage 1, mean displacement covariate, Z > 2.3, and corrected cluster p < 0.05. The bistable perception experiment involved 26 participants completing four 120 s trials for each of two stimuli in randomized order, with eye assignment switched on alternate trials. Binocular rivalry used oblique gratings of 2 cycles per degree, 50 percent contrast, plus or minus 45 degrees, 2.3 degrees in diameter, and a raised cosine envelope, shown to opposite eyes. The Necker cube had a face width of 2 degrees and no binocular disparity. Stimuli were displayed on a gamma-corrected CRT monitor with mean luminance 32 cd/m2, viewed through a mirror stereoscope, surrounded by a dark ring and binocular Voronoi texture. Participants reported percepts by holding one mouse button for one percept and the other button for the alternative percept, and released both buttons when undecided. Geometric mean dominance durations were used because dominance durations followed a positively skewed gamma distribution.
[end related work 1]

