[method]
The paper used a permutation-based classification analysis on fMRI-derived statistical maps and independent component analysis features. For 26-way task classification, run 1 whole-brain data were regressed against spatial independent component analysis components estimated from run 2 to separate training and test data. Independent component analysis was estimated at multiple dimensionality levels from 2 to 200 components, and component loadings were used as classification features. Three classifiers were evaluated: linear support vector machine, nonlinear radial-basis-function support vector machine, and L2-penalized logistic regression. Classifier parameters were optimized on run 2 data, and classification accuracy was evaluated on run 1 data using leave-one-out cross-validation. An empirical null distribution was obtained by repeating the classification 1000 times with randomly permuted labels. Additional analyses used whole-brain Z-statistic maps with a linear support vector machine and eight-fold balanced cross-validation, and subject identification with a one-vs-all linear support vector machine tested on run 2 data. This method relates to the proposed research plan because it uses permutation testing to establish statistical significance for fMRI-derived analyses, although it tests classification accuracy rather than voxel or cluster activation and does not apply cluster-level family-wise error correction.
[results]
For 26-way task classification, accuracy increased as the number of independent components rose from 2 to 100 and then remained relatively stable around 50 percent. The two linear classifiers performed similarly, and the nonlinear support vector machine was only slightly higher, with all classification values substantially greater than chance. Whole-brain Z-statistic map classification with a linear support vector machine yielded moderate accuracy of 48.8 percent, compared with a 95th percentile null value of 7.7 percent. Subject identification from statistical maps achieved 66.9 percent accuracy on run 2 data, while random subject relabeling produced a mean accuracy of 0.3 percent and a 95th percentile null value of 0.9 percent.
[context]
The analysis was performed on OpenFMRI task fMRI datasets comprising multiple studies and 337 subjects, with 26 task classes. Some subjects contributed data to multiple datasets, introducing non-independence that likely reduced task classification accuracy. Whole-brain Z-statistic analysis excluded voxels missing for more than 3 subjects, leaving 174,264 voxels. Subject classification included only voxels with non-zero values for all subjects, leaving 152,704 voxels, and generalization was tested on run 2 data for 317 of 337 subjects. Independent component analysis dimensionality ranged from 2 to 200 components, task classification nulls used 1000 label permutations, and subject classification nulls used random subject relabeling. The broader processing pipeline used FSL and FreeSurfer on a high-performance cluster, but the permutation analysis itself was a post-hoc classification evaluation rather than a primary cluster-level fMRI inference procedure.
[end related work 2]

Processing method: To make statistical inferences for the fMRI results: Statistical inference will use permutation testing with cluster-level family-wise error correction.

