  "support": "The strongest support from the related work is that fMRI can reveal duration-specific activity in parietal cortex, which is directly relevant to the proposed prediction that parietal regions encode temporal variables in a perspective-dependent manner. [(Hayashi et al., 2018-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c3d9101426abdb68a07d4f49760f77c5b1d795cf) used functional localizer and main duration-task scans, general linear model analyses, and searchlight multivoxel pattern analysis to report that frontal and parietal ROIs carried duration information while an occipital ROI carried orientation information. This supports the proposed use of fMRI, ROI-based and searchlight analyses, and the broader assumption that parietal cortex represents temporal duration rather than only spatial or visual features. A weaker but still relevant support is the paper's separation of duration from orientation, which parallels the proposed need to distinguish temporal variables such as sequential distance and event duration from non-temporal linguistic or visual confounds such as syllable length and phrase identity. The weakest support is general: the study demonstrates that temporal duration can be neurally decoded in parietal regions, but it does not test the proposed complementary parietal-hippocampal account, internal versus external temporal perspectives, or interaction effects between task perspective and temporal variables, so it cannot directly validate the planned parametric modulation of sequential distance and duration in the Italian ritual paradigm.",
